
; ======================================================================
;  THE NETWORK - net, get and put
; ======================================================================
;  This is the caller half of three libraries that deliberately do no
;  input and no output of their own:
;
;    RaspberryPi4/Lib/genet.pi4  the Ethernet MAC. Proven on silicon on
;                                2026-08-26 up to and including receive
;                                at 1000 Mbit/s full duplex.
;    RaspberryPi4/Lib/net.pi4    ARP, IPv4, ICMP and UDP. It builds and
;                                parses frames and never touches a
;                                register.
;    RaspberryPi4/Lib/tftp.pi4   RFC 1350, client side. It builds and
;                                parses TFTP payloads and does not know
;                                that the other two exist.
;
;  Each of them states the same contract in its own header - net.pi4:18
;  and tftp.pi4:27 - and the reason is worth restating once here,
;  because this file is where the cost of that contract is paid. A
;  library that did its own I/O could not be gated: tools/a64/
;  a64_tftp_check.py feeds byte-exact payloads in and reads byte-exact
;  payloads out with no device model underneath, and that is only
;  possible because there is nothing underneath. The price is that
;  somebody has to run the pump, and Anvil is that somebody.
;
;  THE PUMP, IN ONE LINE, and it is the same one tftp.pi4:66-104 sets
;  out:
;
;      GenetRecvWait -> NetInput -> TftpInput -> NetUdpBuild -> GenetSend
;
;  EthPump() below is that line with the counters and the refusals
;  attached, and EthRun() is the loop around it.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT ARRIVES BEFORE THE FIRST BLOCK DOES, AND WHY IT IS HERE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4TftpProbe.pi4 sends its read
;  request the instant TftpBeginRead() has staged it. On a bench where
;  the server has already ARPed the board that works, and on a bench
;  where it has not, NetUdpBuild() refuses with #NET_E_NO_ARP
;  (net.pi4:469-471, raised at net.pi4:1308) and the probe prints a send
;  failure it cannot explain.
;
;  A UDP DATAGRAM CANNOT BE BUILT UNTIL THE NEXT HOP'S HARDWARE ADDRESS
;  IS KNOWN. net.pi4 does not resolve one for you and it is right not
;  to: resolving means sending, waiting and receiving, which is I/O, and
;  I/O is the caller's. So EthResolve() below does it - one ARP request
;  to the address NetNextHop() names, then listen, then ask the cache
;  again - before either transfer command builds anything.
;
;  AND THE ENTRY CAN DIE MID-CONVERSATION. net.pi4 gained
;  net_ConfirmNeighbour() (net.pi4:1391), called from net_RecvIp
;  (net.pi4:2106), for exactly that reason: until 2026-08-26 an ARP
;  entry expired on a fixed twenty-second timer no matter how recently
;  the neighbour had been heard from, so a transfer longer than the
;  lifetime lost its next hop halfway through. Every frame this pump
;  hands to NetInput() therefore pushes the deadline out as a side
;  effect, which is why the pump must keep calling NetInput() even for
;  frames it has no interest in. Do not "optimise" that away.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE THREE RULES FROM RFC 1350 AND RFC 1123 THAT THIS LOOP MUST OBEY
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  tftp.pi4 implements all three. They are restated here because the
;  pump can break every one of them from the outside.
;
;  1. THE TRANSFER IDENTIFIER. The read request goes to port 69 and the
;     answer DOES NOT COME FROM PORT 69: "the response to the request,
;     under normal operation, uses a TID chosen by the server as its
;     source TID" [Reference/rfc1350.txt:197-210, and the worked example
;     at :487-491]. tftp.pi4 adopts the source port of the first DATA
;     packet and sends everything afterwards there. The pump breaks this
;     by CACHING a destination: it must read TftpOutIp() and
;     TftpOutPort() on every single send, because the one packet that
;     does not go to the server is the ERROR 5 sent to a stranger who
;     used the wrong identifier [rfc1350.txt:239-246], and sending that
;     to the server would tell the server its own transfer had failed.
;     EthSendStaged() reads both, every time, and that is not an
;     accident of style.
;
;  2. THE SORCERER'S APPRENTICE. "Implementations MUST contain the fix
;     for this problem: the sender ... must never resend the current
;     DATA packet on receipt of a duplicate ACK"
;     [Reference/rfc1123.txt:2589-2603]. tftp.pi4 stages NOTHING for a
;     duplicate, in both directions. The pump breaks this by sending
;     something anyway - which is what "send the staging buffer after
;     every packet" would do if the buffer were not cleared. It is
;     cleared, by TftpInput() and by TftpTick() both, and
;     EthSendStaged() returns immediately when TftpOutLen() is zero. A
;     pump that ignored that length would put every packet on the wire
;     twice for the rest of the transfer.
;
;  3. AND THEREFORE THE TIMER IS NOT OPTIONAL. A receiver that is silent
;     on duplicates and has no retransmit timer hangs forever the first
;     time an acknowledgement is lost - both ends being polite at each
;     other until the server gives up. TftpTick() is the other half of
;     rule 2 and deleting it from this loop is not a simplification, it
;     is the reintroduction of a hang. tftp.pi4:1916 and its header at
;     :360-372 say the same at more length, and the library's gate has a
;     mutation for it.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE ETHERNET RECEIVE REGION - HALF A MEGABYTE THAT IS NOT A GLOBAL
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  genet.pi4 requires a receive region of #GENET_RX_TOTAL_BYTES, which
;  is 524288 - 256 descriptors of 2048 bytes [genet.pi4:1013, from
;  U-Boot's driver at U:181]. It has to be real DRAM, 64-byte aligned
;  [genet.pi4:2196], and the MAC writes into it by DMA whenever the
;  engine is running.
;
;  IT IS NOT A Global Dim, AND THE ARGUMENT THAT CHOSE THAT IS NO LONGER
;  TRUE. It is recorded here in full rather than deleted, because the
;  next question "why is this not just an array" deserves the real
;  history and not a decision with nothing behind it.
;
;  WHAT IT SAID, when it was written: a 512 KiB array would land in this
;  image's BSS, BSS sat inside #MON_LO..#MON_HI - the two-megabyte window
;  the monitor reserved for itself - and it ran to about $310000 against
;  an autoboot record at $3FFF00. So the array would fit, with roughly
;  half a megabyte to spare, and then one day it would not, and the first
;  thing it would overwrite is the autoboot record, silently.
;
;  BOTH HALVES OF THAT WENT AWAY. On 2026-08-28 BSS left the image window
;  for its own region. The original 8 MiB BSS range later proved too small
;  after graphics integration, so current BssAddress/#MON_DATA reserve
;  $0D000000..$0DFFFFFF (16 MiB). The emitted map gate checks that globals
;  fit the reservation as graphics integrations change. On 2026-09-08 the
;  window itself went: the
;  monitor has no maximum size and the autoboot record moved to #AB_BASE
;  $001FF000, BELOW the load address, where neither the image nor BSS can
;  reach it at any size. The silent failure this paragraph was avoiding
;  cannot happen any more, from either direction.
;
;  SO WHY IS IT STILL A FIXED ADDRESS? Because moving a live DMA region is
;  a change, and there is no longer a reason FOR one either way - which is
;  a fair answer for today and not a good one to leave standing. What is
;  still true of #ETH_RX_BASE ($08100000) is written below and is worth
;  keeping: it is outside both payload windows, so no image the monitor
;  can be asked to load can be aimed at it. A Global Dim would land in
;  #MON_DATA_LO..#MON_DATA_HI, which HitsMonitor() also refuses, so that
;  property is not unique to it. If this file is open for another reason,
;  this is the paragraph to settle.
;
;  SO IT IS A FIXED ADDRESS IN THE BAND THE MEMORY MAP ALREADY REFUSES
;  TO OFFER. $08100000 is the first byte above the payload stack
;  corridor ($07F00000..$080FFFFF) and it is nowhere near either payload
;  window - #PAY0_HI is $07EFFFFF - so no image the monitor will load
;  can be aimed at it and no range check has to be taught about it.
;  Bank 0 runs to $3B2FFFFF, so there is real DRAM there.
;
;  IT IS NOT PROTECTED FROM A PAYLOAD, and cannot be: a payload can
;  write anywhere. What IS done is that the DMA engine is stopped before
;  control leaves the monitor - see the EthPayloadQuiesce() call in
;  RunAt() - so a running payload is never sharing memory with a live
;  bus master.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE FOUR ADDRESSES LIVE IN THE SETTINGS STORE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  U-Boot keeps ipaddr, netmask, gatewayip and serverip in its
;  environment [Reference/v2025.01_cmd_net.c:215-243], and the reason is
;  the same one that applies here: nobody wants to retype four addresses
;  after every reset. Anvil already has an environment - the KEY=VALUE
;  store behind `settings` - so these go in it, under net.address,
;  net.netmask, net.gateway and net.server.
;
;  THE NAMES ARE NOT U-BOOT'S. Anvil's store namespaces its keys with a
;  dot (wifi.network, wifi.password.plaintext) and a flat `ipaddr`
;  sitting beside those would be the odd one out. The help text names
;  U-Boot's spellings so a person coming from U-Boot can find them.
;
;  NOTHING LOADS THE STORE FOR YOU. `settings load` reads it and
;  `settings save` writes it, exactly as for the Wi-Fi keys, and `net`
;  says so rather than letting an operator discover after a reset that
;  the addresses went away.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT HAS NOT BEEN PROVED, SAID BEFORE IT IS DISCOVERED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE ETHERNET CABLE IS NOT IN THE BOARD. It was unplugged after
;  transmit and receive were proven on 2026-08-26 and nothing in this
;  section has moved a single frame. Every claim here is a reading of
;  the three libraries and of the RFCs beside them, and not a bench
;  claim. What that means in practice:
;
;    * no end-to-end transfer has happened, in either direction;
;    * the ARP resolve has never had a reply to parse;
;    * the retransmit path has never had a packet to lose;
;    * the mailbox MAC-address read has never been compared against the
;      address printed on a real board.
;
;  The one thing that IS covered without a cable is the refusal: with no
;  link, GenetPhyWaitLink() returns #GENET_ERR_NO_LINK inside
;  #ETH_LINK_MS and EthUp() says so in a whole sentence rather than
;  sitting there. That was the requirement and it is the only part of
;  this that a dark port can demonstrate.
; ======================================================================

; --- the receive region. See THE ETHERNET RECEIVE REGION above. -------
#ETH_RX_BASE  = $08100000      ; 64-byte aligned, as genet.pi4:2196
                               ; requires, and outside both payload
                               ; windows on purpose
#ETH_RX_BYTES = 524288         ; #GENET_RX_TOTAL_BYTES, genet.pi4:1013

; The largest frame the pump will accept into its own buffer.
; genet.pi4 copies at most #GENET_RX_BUF_LENGTH - #GENET_RX_BUF_OFFSET
; bytes, which is 2046 [genet.pi4:1010-1011], and refuses with
; #GENET_ERR_RX_LONG rather than truncating if the capacity is smaller
; than the descriptor's length. 2048 is therefore the number that can
; never produce that refusal.
#ETH_FRAME_MAX = 2048

; How long to wait for auto-negotiation. THERE IS NO CITABLE NUMBER for
; this and genet.pi4:1888-1897 says so at length - U-Boot's own value
; lives in a Kconfig this project has no copy of. What that note does
; give is a measurement: "gigabit auto-negotiation takes on the order of
; two to four seconds from a cold link, and rather longer if the far end
; is an old switch that does parallel detection". Five seconds covers
; the stated range with a second of margin, and it is short enough that
; a dark port answers while somebody is still looking at the terminal.
#ETH_LINK_MS = 5000

; How long one frame may take to leave. genet.pi4's GenetSend() takes a
; real deadline rather than a spin count for the reason its header
; records; 100 ms is four orders of magnitude more than a 1518-byte
; frame needs at a gigabit and is a stuck-engine detector, not a flow
; control.
#ETH_TX_MS = 100

; One slice of the pump. Short enough that a keypress is noticed
; promptly - OutBreak() is only asked once per slice - and long enough
; that the loop is not spinning on an empty ring. A DURATION AND NOT A
; POLL COUNT, which is the mistake genet.pi4:3060-3078 records costing a
; hardware run: eight calls to GenetRecv() on an empty ring is a few
; microseconds and reads exactly like a receive path that does not work.
#ETH_SLICE_MS = 200

; The ARP resolve: eight tries, half a second of listening each, so four
; seconds before it gives up. A neighbour that has not answered in four
; seconds is not going to.
#ETH_ARP_MS    = 500
#ETH_ARP_TRIES = 8

; The TFTP retransmit timer handed to TftpSetTimeout(). Two seconds and
; eight tries is sixteen seconds of patience before the library fails
; the transfer itself with #TFTP_E_TIMEOUT. The library's own defaults
; are 5000 and 10 [tftp.pi4:628-629]; these are shorter because there is
; a person watching the prompt.
#ETH_RETRY_MS = 2000
#ETH_RETRIES  = 8

; The absolute ceiling on one transfer, and it is a backstop rather than
; a policy. The retransmit budget above ends a DEAD transfer in sixteen
; seconds; this ends a transfer that is somehow making just enough
; progress to keep resetting that budget and never finishing. Ten
; minutes is far longer than any legitimate transfer this board can be
; asked for - 512-byte blocks at a gigabit move a four-megabyte file in
; seconds - and a keypress stops it long before this does.
#ETH_MAX_MS = 600000

; A dot on the terminal every this many blocks, so a long transfer looks
; alive. 64 blocks is 32768 bytes, which is a dot roughly every ten
; lines' worth of a 260 KB image - enough to see it moving, few enough
; that the HDMI console is not being repainted for a progress indicator.
#ETH_DOT_BLOCKS = 64

Global Dim gEthMac.a[6]        ; this board's hardware address
Global Dim gEthPeer.a[6]       ; whatever NetArpLookup last resolved
Global Dim gEthRx.a[2048]      ; #ETH_FRAME_MAX. One frame at a time -
                               ; the ring itself is at #ETH_RX_BASE and
                               ; genet.pi4 copies out of it into here.

Global gEthIp.i                ; the four addresses, parsed out of the
Global gEthMask.i              ; settings store by EthConfig()
Global gEthGw.i
Global gEthSrv.i

; WHERE THE ADDRESS THE LINK IS ACTUALLY CARRYING CAME FROM. One of the
; #NET_ADDR_* codes in Anvil/Core/netcfg.pbi, written only by
; NetAddressBound and read by `net` and `net link`.
;
; IT IS NOT COSMETIC. A lease and a stored address behave identically
; afterwards - the same three numbers in the same IP layer, the same
; console on the same port, arrived at by the same path - and that
; sameness is the whole design of 2026-09-07. But they FAIL differently,
; and an operator looking at a board that has stopped answering needs to
; know which one this is: a lease that stopped renewing is a question
; about a server, and a stored address nobody else on the segment agrees
; about is a question about the settings. A monitor that printed
; 192.168.137.2 for both would be no help with either.
Global gEthAddrFrom.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE WIRED LINK-LOCAL ADDRESS - 0 when there is none.  2026-09-07.
;
;  WHY IT IS A SEPARATE WORD AND NOT JUST gEthIp. The three addresses
;  above are RE-READ OUT OF THE SETTINGS STORE by EthHasIpConfig()
;  before every interface choice, deliberately - see the reasoning in
;  HwLinkOpen, and it is right: an operator who retypes `net server`
;  between two transfers must not be ignored. A link-local address is
;  the one address on this board that is NOT in the settings store and
;  MUST NOT BE PUT THERE:
;
;    * it is not a saved static address. It was never agreed by anyone,
;      it belongs to one segment on one evening, and writing it into
;      SETTINGS.TXT would make the next boot treat it as the operator's
;      considered choice - which would then WIN over the link-local
;      machinery and be re-used without a single probe. An address taken
;      on the strength of a probe that was never run is exactly the
;      collision RFC 3927 exists to prevent.
;    * it would silently displace a real static address somebody had set
;      for a different network.
;
;  So it lives here, in memory only, and EthHasIpConfig() falls back to
;  it when the settings store has nothing. That one fallback is what
;  makes every path downstream - the interface preference order, the
;  console arming, `net`, `net link` - treat a link-local address
;  exactly like any other, which is the rule of
;  Memories\the-console-is-networking-not-a-driver-feature.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global gEthLlIp.i

Global gEthUp.i                ; 1 once GenetStart() has succeeded and
                               ; net.pi4 has been told who we are
Global gEthMacKnown.i          ; 1 once gEthMac holds something
Global gEthMacFirmware.i       ; 1 if the firmware gave us the address,
                               ; 0 if we fell back to a made-up one

Global gEthFrames.i            ; the tally, reset at the start of every
Global gEthUdp.i               ; transfer
Global gEthArpOut.i
Global gEthSendFail.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The four settings keys, spelled once each. See THE FOUR ADDRESSES
;  above for why they are not called ipaddr and serverip.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
